  {
   "slug": "first-week-of-strength-training",
   "title": "Your first week of strength training",
   "date": "2026-10-09T07:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "workouts"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "strength",
    "beginner"
   ],
   "featured_media": "barbell",
   "excerpt": "Three sessions, three big lifts, zero ego. A calm start that builds habits before it builds max weights.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The first week is not about lifting heavy. It is about learning the movements, finding your starting weights and leaving the gym wanting to come back.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Monday</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Squat 3 × 8</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Push-up 3 × 10</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Plank 3 × 30 s</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Wednesday</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Deadlift 3 × 6</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Row 3 × 10</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dead bug 3 × 10</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Friday</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Squat 3 × 8</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Overhead press 3 × 8</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Carry 3 × 40 m</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Three rules</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Stop every set with two good reps left in the tank.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Add a little weight only when every rep looks the same.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Sleep counts as training.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Everyone in the gym started with an empty bar. Nobody is watching you as closely as you think.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Head coach</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:plates}}\" alt=\"Weight plates with lime rings\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "spin-class-survival-guide",
   "title": "Spin class survival guide",
   "date": "2026-09-29T18:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "workouts"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "cardio",
    "beginner"
   ],
   "featured_media": "spin",
   "excerpt": "Bike set-up, what the numbers mean and how to finish your first class smiling.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Spin is loud, dark and brilliant fun. A few minutes of preparation make the difference between loving it and limping out.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Set up the bike</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Saddle at hip height when you stand beside the bike.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Slight bend in the knee at the bottom of the pedal stroke.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Handlebars level with the saddle for your first class.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Feels like</th><th>You can…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–2</td><td>Easy</td><td>Chat in full sentences</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Steady</td><td>Speak a few words</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Hard</td><td>Grunt</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>All out</td><td>Only breathe</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bring water, a small towel and arrive ten minutes early so the instructor can check your set-up.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:heartbeat}}\" alt=\"A glowing heart-rate line\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most of a class sits in zones 2 to 4.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "eating-around-early-workouts",
   "title": "Eating around early workouts",
   "date": "2026-09-18T06:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "nutrition"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "beginner"
   ],
   "featured_media": "bottle",
   "excerpt": "Train at 6am? Here is what to eat before, after and the night before, without a meal plan.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Early sessions are great for consistency, but an empty tank can make them feel brutal. You do not need special products, just a little planning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} --><h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The night before</h4><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A normal dinner with carbohydrates</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A big glass of water</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} --><h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before (if you can)</h4><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Half a banana or a slice of toast</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Coffee is fine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} --><h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">After</h4><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Protein and carbs within two hours</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Eggs on toast, yogurt and fruit</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your best pre-workout is last night's sleep.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Studio nutrition coach</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you feel dizzy or sick training fasted, eat something small. Listen to your body before you listen to the internet.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "why-rest-days-build-muscle",
   "title": "Why rest days build muscle",
   "date": "2026-09-07T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "recovery"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "mobility"
   ],
   "featured_media": "yoga",
   "excerpt": "Training breaks muscle down; rest builds it back stronger. How to plan recovery like a pro.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Muscles do not grow during a workout. They grow afterwards, while you sleep, eat and move gently. Skip the rest and you skip the results.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">A good rest day</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A 30-minute walk or easy ride</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ten minutes of mobility work</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Protein at every meal</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Eight hours in bed</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:yoga}}\" alt=\"A yoga mat with blocks\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Our Sunday mobility class is built for rest days.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Two or three rest days a week is normal for most people. Feeling flat for days, sleeping badly or picking up niggles are signs you need more.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "battle-ropes-three-finishers",
   "title": "Battle ropes: three finishers",
   "date": "2026-08-25T17:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "workouts"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "hiit",
    "cardio"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ropes",
   "excerpt": "Thirty seconds on, thirty off. Three rope finishers to end any session on a high.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ropes are the most honest piece of kit in the gym: they move exactly as hard as you do. Add one of these finishers to the end of a session.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"className\":\"workout\"} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list workout\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Waves</strong> Alternate arms, 30 seconds on, 30 off, × 6.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Slams</strong> Both arms overhead and down, 20 seconds on, 40 off, × 6.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Circles</strong> Draw big circles, switch direction every round, × 6.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Keep your knees soft and your back flat. The power comes from your legs and hips, not just your arms.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "sunday-open-track-sessions",
   "title": "New: Sunday open track sessions",
   "date": "2026-08-12T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "studio-news"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "cardio"
   ],
   "featured_media": "track",
   "excerpt": "From September, members can join coached running sessions at the city track every Sunday morning.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We are taking training outside. Every Sunday at 8:00, one of our coaches will run a free session at the city track for members of every level.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Warm-up and running drills</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Intervals in three pace groups</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cool-down and stretching</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bring trainers and water. No booking needed; just turn up and find the lime flag.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:trainers}}\" alt=\"Three coaches standing together\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Our coaches lead every Sunday session.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->",
   "format": "standard"
  },
  {
   "slug": "reading-heart-rate-zones",
   "title": "How to read your heart-rate zones",
   "date": "2026-07-30T07:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "recovery",
    "workouts"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "cardio"
   ],
   "featured_media": "heartbeat",
   "excerpt": "Five zones, one simple idea: most training should feel easier than you think.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Heart-rate zones help you train at the right effort on the right day. The simplest way to find yours is a rough maximum of 220 minus your age, then work out the percentages.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>% of max</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>50–60%</td><td>Warm-ups and recovery</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>60–70%</td><td>Building your base</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>70–80%</td><td>Steady endurance</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>80–90%</td><td>Hard intervals</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>90–100%</td><td>Short, all-out efforts</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A good week spends most of its time in zones 1 and 2, with small doses of 4 and 5. Easy days easy, hard days hard.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Train slow to get fast. It sounds wrong until you try it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Endurance coach</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "format": "standard"
  }
 ],
